{
  "diagnosis": "The episode failed before a grasp was established: no gripper-close command was issued. Early depth probes provided plausible target and support geometry, but later depth use was unreliable. At steps 6–8, the head image did not show the mug, yet returned points were interpreted as its rim or side. This suggests cross-camera correspondence errors; non-null depth alone did not establish feature identity. Repeated open-hand approaches displaced and tilted the mug instead of establishing verified opposing contacts. Retreat eventually relieved contact, but the policy repeatedly adopted measured orientation drift rather than maintaining a separate task-attitude reference. After losing the target in all views, it rotated the wrist to search without establishing clearance for the entire hand's sweep. The final search rotation toppled the mug. The evaluator reported only 7.31 cm of lift, no upright grasp, and no sustained retention. Repeated probing, approach–retreat motions, and unsuccessful viewing adjustments consumed substantial effort without progressing to closure.",
  "failure_mode": "Unvalidated depth correspondence and loss of visual localization led to misaligned contact, accumulated attitude drift, and an unsafe wrist-search sweep before grasp closure.",
  "lessons_review": [
    {
      "lesson": "Plan close manipulation using the measured geometry of the entire hand, any held object, and their swept paths, not just palm position or support height. Before closure, use images and proprioception to place the object inside the actual grasp aperture with opposing contact surfaces aligned. If an unsecured object shifts or tilts during entry, use a short contact-relief motion that visibly opens a gap while preserving support, then reobserve and change the entry alignment. If the object follows, revise the separating direction. Once the object is visibly bracketed for closure, hold the palm pose and close rather than pushing farther.",
      "verdict": "helped",
      "why": "Contact relief had a limited benefit: step 8 ended without mug contact, and the mug was upright at step 9. However, earlier retreat and re-entry did not establish a reliably changed alignment. The whole-hand swept-path requirement was also not applied to the later viewing rotations. Keep the principle, while making changed alignment and clearance before re-entry more explicit."
    },
    {
      "lesson": "After each motion, compare the measured palm pose and visible contact geometry with the requested result. If tracking worsens, motion stalls, or the grasp aperture moves differently than intended, inspect the available views instead of repeating the same approach. Relieve obstructing contact while preserving support; if contact is not evident, change to a clearance-checked approach pose and test a smaller motion. Construct incremental commands from the measured pose, while retaining a separate visually verified task-attitude reference rather than replacing it with tracking drift.",
      "verdict": "neutral",
      "why": "The policy generally constructed translations from measured positions, but repeatedly copied the reached quaternion despite appreciable orientation errors, especially at steps 6, 8, and 9. It did not preserve the separate attitude reference. Step 11 appropriately changed position after the collision rejection, but subsequent viewing rotations still lacked target clearance. Strengthen the connection between tracking drift, lost visibility, and safe viewpoint recovery."
    },
    {
      "lesson": "Use depth only for identifiable features in the current image from the camera supplying that depth, with its pixel convention; do not transfer pixels directly from another camera. Reconcile each return with visible target or support features and the measured hand before using it to choose contact placement or a motion path. If the relevant feature is visible only in another view, use that view to guide a clearance-checked adjustment that restores reliable depth correspondence, or choose an action supported by the available visual geometry. Probe the uncertainty that changes the next action and reuse geometry that remains valid.",
      "verdict": "neutral",
      "why": "Early probes helped estimate support and target-surface geometry, but source-image correspondence was not consistently enforced. Small pixel selections at steps 3–8 appear associated with wrist-view features, and target-specific depth interpretations continued after the head view lost the mug. Step 10 also sampled depth without an identifiable target. The principle remains necessary; explicitly distinguish a valid return from a correctly identified feature."
    },
    {
      "lesson": "After grasping or changing contact, verify retention, target motion relative to support, and target attitude separately using visible features and a short task-compatible test motion. If features are ambiguous, inspect an informative camera or make a clearance-checked viewing adjustment before continuing. If seating changes, guide the object to stable support and reseat; if seating is stable but attitude is wrong, correct toward the preserved task-compatible attitude with observed motions. Begin or resume a required hold only after its conditions are visibly established, and monitor them throughout.",
      "verdict": "neutral",
      "why": "No grasp was closed, so retention and hold verification were never reached. The evaluator's incidental lift did not satisfy either uprightness or retention. Keep this safeguard, but make clear that viewing adjustments themselves require swept-path clearance."
    }
  ],
  "lessons": [
    {
      "lesson": "Probe depth only on an identifiable feature in the current image from the camera supplying it, using that image's pixel convention. Reconcile each return with the visible feature, support, and measured hand before using it; a valid return does not establish target identity. If correspondence is ambiguous, use an informative RGB view to choose a clearance-checked action, restoring depth-source visibility when needed rather than transferring pixels or sampling blindly. Reuse verified geometry until observed motion makes it stale, and probe only uncertainty that changes the next action.",
      "evidence_steps": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 10],
      "why": "Initial depth information was useful, but later target interpretations were incompatible with the head camera's loss of the mug. Unsupported probing could not resolve grasp alignment and added cost. Explicit feature identity would have prevented treating unrelated or ambiguous returns as contact geometry."
    },
    {
      "lesson": "For grasp acquisition, place the intended contact region inside the actual grasp aperture with visible opposing surfaces aligned, using images and measured hand geometry rather than palm proximity alone. If the unsecured object shifts or tilts during entry, make a small separating motion that visibly reduces contact while preserving support, then change alignment before re-entry. If the object follows, revise the separating direction. Once the target is visibly bracketed, hold the palm pose and close, then test retention rather than advancing farther.",
      "evidence_steps": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8],
      "why": "Open-hand entry repeatedly contacted and tilted the mug, reaching substantial tilt before any closure. Step 6's withdrawal was followed by another contacting approach at step 7. Step 8 finally separated the hand, demonstrating that relief was possible, but relief needed to lead to a revised grasp alignment rather than another approach cycle."
    },
    {
      "lesson": "After each motion, compare measured pose and visible geometry with the intended result. Build increments from measured state while preserving a separate visually verified task-attitude reference. When tracking worsens or the target leaves view, inspect the other views and use the last verified geometry, allowing for uncertainty, to move the entire hand into visibly clear space. Check the swept paths of the hand, arm, and any held object before rotating to search or restore attitude; a stationary palm does not guarantee clearance. If visibility or clearance does not improve, revise the viewing or approach direction instead of repeating the same adjustment.",
      "evidence_steps": [6, 7, 8, 9, 10, 11, 12, 13],
      "why": "Repeatedly adopting reached orientations accumulated drift and degraded visibility. The viewing rotation at step 10 was rejected for body clearance; repositioning at step 11 addressed that obstruction but did not establish target clearance. Rotations at steps 12–13 contacted the unseen mug, with the final action producing a 112.4-degree tilt and substantial unintended palm displacement."
    },
    {
      "lesson": "After grasping or changing contact, verify retention, object motion relative to support, and task-compatible attitude separately using visible features and a short task-compatible test motion. If evidence is ambiguous, select an informative view or make a swept-path-checked viewing adjustment. If the object slips or changes seating, guide it to stable support and reseat; if seating is secure but attitude is wrong, correct toward the preserved task attitude with observed motions. Begin any required hold only after its conditions are visibly established, and monitor them throughout.",
      "evidence_steps": [3, 5, 7, 13],
      "why": "Contact produced height changes without a closed grasp, and the evaluator's reported lift was neither sufficiently high nor upright and retained. Separating these checks prevents incidental object motion from being mistaken for successful manipulation once contact or closure occurs."
    }
  ]
}
